  "dimensions": [
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_cc8a2773",
      "@type": "OverallIntent",
      "instance_name": "overallintent_instance",
      "label": "the overallintent",
      "description": "meme-id: tffw-your-friend-says-that-kirk-is-better-than-picard; hasOverallIntent: Harmful; overall-intent-description: The image depicts a man in nerdy attire (glasses, bow tie, suspenders) standing in front of a chalkboard with mathematical and geometric equations, smiling broadly. The text overlay reads 'tffw your friend says that kirk is better than picard'. This meme is a humorous reaction to a common debate among Star Trek fans about which captain — Captain James T. Kirk or Captain Jean-Luc Picard — is superior. The visual of the 'nerd' character is juxtaposed with the text to imply that someone who is overly passionate or 'obsessed' with this debate is being portrayed as a stereotypical 'nerd' or 'geek'. While the meme is not explicitly abusive, cyberbullying, or hateful toward any specific protected group, it does rely on a stereotype — portraying passionate fans of a particular fandom as 'nerds' — which can be perceived as mildly condescending or dismissive. The humor is derived from the contrast between the earnest, intellectual appearance of the character and the trivial nature of the debate. The meme's intent is to mock or poke fun at the passionate fandom debate, but it does not contain overtly harmful or toxic content. However, the stereotype of 'nerds' as being overly passionate or obsessive about niche topics can be seen as a form of mild social ridicule, which may be considered 'Harmful' in the sense that it could potentially make some fans feel belittled or marginalized, even if unintentionally.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_e3df5b95"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
