    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_606ef364",
      "@type": "AnalogicalMapping",
      "instance_name": "mapping_001",
      "label": "The 'nerd' character is mapped onto the person who is passionately defending the opinion that Kirk is better than Picard.",
      "description": "The text 'tffw your friend says that kirk is better than picard' implies that the person in the image (the 'nerd' character) is the one experiencing the 'that feeling when' scenario — specifically, the feeling of being confronted by a friend who holds the opinion that Kirk is superior to Picard. The visual of the smiling, nerdy-looking man is used to represent this passionate fan. The mapping is established by the meme format 'tffw' which projects the viewer's emotional state onto the visual character, and the text explicitly identifies the character's role as someone reacting to this specific fandom debate. The visual material (the man) is thus mapped onto the textual material (the opinion about Kirk vs. Picard) to create a humorous stereotype.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_e3df5b95"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_b6566dd7",
      "@type": "SemioticProjection",
      "instance_name": "semiotic_projection_1",
      "label": "User projected onto the nerdy man character reacting to the fandom debate",
      "description": "The user is projected onto the visual material of the man (the 'nerd' character) who is smiling broadly in front of the chalkboard. This projection is established by the 'tffw' meme format, which stands for 'That Feeling When Your Friend Says'. The text explicitly references 'your friend', which directly deictically points to the user as the one whose friend is making the statement. The user is thus positioned as the person experiencing the feeling described — the feeling of being confronted by a friend who holds the opinion that Kirk is better than Picard. The visual of the nerdy man is used to represent this user's emotional or social state in the context of the fandom debate. The projection is onto the VisualMaterial entity 'man' and is mediated by the TextualMaterial 'tffw your friend says'.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_e3df5b95"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_be9070cc",
      "@type": "ToxicityAssessment",
      "instance_name": "toxicity_1",
      "label": "Harmful",
      "description": "The meme relies on a stereotype — portraying passionate fans of a particular fandom as 'nerds' — which can be perceived as mildly condescending or dismissive. The humor is derived from the contrast between the earnest, intellectual appearance of the character and the trivial nature of the debate. The meme's intent is to mock or poke fun at the passionate fandom debate, but it does not contain overtly harmful or toxic content. However, the stereotype of 'nerds' as being overly passionate or obsessive about niche topics can be seen as a form of mild social ridicule, which may be considered 'Harmful' in the sense that it could potentially make some fans feel belittled or marginalized, even if unintentionally.",
